(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-2-cms-exam-2-master-exam-form-d"]={"title":"CMS I Exam 2 Master Exam — Form D","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","exam":"Exam 2","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"A 62-year-old man is found on screening to have an intraocular pressure of 26 mm Hg in both eyes with an enlarged optic cup. He has no symptoms. Which visual field is lost first?","choices":["The superior field only","The central field","The peripheral field","The temporal field of one eye only"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Central acuity is preserved until late, which is why it is found on screening rather than by the patient. — 12. Acute Vision Loss, Slide 18"},{"q":"What is the prognosis of herpes simplex keratitis?","choices":["It resolves without any treatment","Good, though recurrences are common","It never recurs after the first episode","Uniformly poor with permanent scarring"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. — CMS I Common Ophthalmological Disorders, Slide 59"},{"q":"A relative afferent pupillary defect is detected. What does its presence indicate about the cause of vision loss?","choices":["The optic nerve or extensive retina","The cornea is scarred","The lens is opaque","The refractive error is uncorrected"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — it separates neural causes from media opacities. — 11. Neuro-Ophthalmology, Slide 26"},{"q":"A 6-year-old boy has a swollen, red right upper and lower lid after a scratch three days ago. The globe is WHITE, eye movements are full and painless, vision is 6/6 and the pupils are equal. Temperature 37.8°C. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Pre-septal cellulitis","Post-septal (orbital) cellulitis","Dacryocystitis","Hordeolum"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. A white eye with full painless movements and normal vision is the single most useful discrimination in this block. — CMS I Common Ophthalmological Disorders, Slide 52"},{"q":"Which structures does slit lamp examination assess?","choices":["The visual pathway posterior to the chiasm","The retina and choroid","The extraocular muscles","The lids, cornea, conjunctiva, sclera and iris"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — the anterior segment. — CMS I Common Ophthalmological Disorders, Slide 9"},{"q":"A 47-year-old man develops floaters, flashes and a curtain over his vision two weeks after blunt ocular trauma. Which type of detachment is most likely?","choices":["Exudative","Traction","Rhegmatogenous","Combined traction and exudative"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — a retinal break allows fluid beneath the retina, and trauma is a common cause. — CMS I Ocular Trauma, Slide 35"},{"q":"A 29-year-old woman with papilledema has a lumbar puncture. What finding is expected?","choices":["Xanthochromia","A raised white cell count","A raised opening pressure with normal constituents","A low opening pressure"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — that is the pattern in idiopathic intracranial hypertension. — 12. Acute Vision Loss, Slide 46"},{"q":"A 34-year-old woman has reduced vision in the left eye. On the swinging flashlight test, both pupils constrict when the light is shone in the right eye, but both DILATE when the light is swung to the left eye. What does this indicate?","choices":["A left relative afferent pupillary defect","Horner syndrome on the left","An efferent defect on the left","An Adie tonic pupil on the left"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The left optic nerve conducts less well, so swinging to it is a net reduction in light input. — 11. Neuro-Ophthalmology, Slide 25"},{"q":"A 2-year-old girl is diagnosed with bilateral retinoblastoma. What does bilateral disease imply?","choices":["That the tumour is benign","That it was caused by an infection","A better prognosis than unilateral disease","A heritable germline mutation"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — bilateral disease is germline, with implications for siblings and for second malignancies. — Chronic Vision Loss & Tumors, Slide 42"},{"q":"How is retinoblastoma managed?","choices":["Observation with annual photographs","Spectacle correction","Multimodal therapy under ocular oncology","Laser trabeculoplasty"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — enucleation for large tumours. With genetic counselling. — Chronic Vision Loss & Tumors, Slide 42"},{"q":"Which patients are named as at risk of bacterial conjunctivitis?","choices":["The immunocompromised, elderly and children","Only patients with allergies","Only those with recent surgery","Only newborns"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Contact lens wearers as well. — CMS I Common Ophthalmological Disorders, Slide 41"},{"q":"A 41-year-old man is found to have a relative afferent pupillary defect. Which of the following would explain it?","choices":["A dense cataract","Refractive error","Optic neuritis","Third nerve palsy"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — a defect of the afferent pathway, which is the optic nerve. — 11. Neuro-Ophthalmology, Slide 26"},{"q":"A 3-year-old boy has an eye that turns inward. The cover test shows the left eye moving out to take up fixation when the right is covered. What is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Patch the deviating eye","Reassure, as children outgrow this","Prescribe reading glasses and review in a year","Refer to ophthalmology"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Manifest misalignment in a child needs assessment to prevent amblyopia. — Chronic Vision Loss & Tumors, Slide 29"},{"q":"In roughly what fraction of FULL-THICKNESS lid lacerations is there an accompanying corneal laceration or globe rupture?","choices":["About one in twenty","Almost all","Almost none","About two thirds"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — which is why the globe is examined in every case. — CMS I Ocular Trauma, Slide 26"},{"q":"Which muscle does the fourth cranial nerve supply, and what is its action?","choices":["Inferior oblique, which elevates the adducted eye","Lateral rectus, which abducts the eye","Superior oblique, which depresses the adducted eye","Medial rectus, which adducts the eye"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — hence vertical diplopia worse on downgaze. — 11. Neuro-Ophthalmology, Slide 43"},{"q":"A 26-year-old woman is diagnosed with adult inclusion conjunctivitis. Besides treating her eye, what else must be addressed?","choices":["Allergen avoidance","Systemic treatment and partner notification","Nothing further; topical treatment suffices","Lid hygiene twice daily"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — the eye is a manifestation of a sexually transmitted infection. The genital infection has to be treated too. — CMS I Common Ophthalmological Disorders, Slide 43"},{"q":"A 60-year-old man has a small, flat, uniformly pigmented choroidal lesion with drusen on its surface and no subretinal fluid. What is the most appropriate management?","choices":["Immediate treatment as a melanoma","Urgent enucleation","Photographic surveillance","Intravitreal anti-VEGF"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Flatness, drusen and absent fluid all favour a benign nevus. — Chronic Vision Loss & Tumors, Slide 49"},{"q":"A 58-year-old woman with a choroidal melanoma asks where it would spread if it did. Which organ is the classic site?","choices":["The liver","The brain","Regional lymph nodes","The lung"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — uveal melanoma spreads haematogenously, and the liver is the classic site. — Chronic Vision Loss & Tumors, Slide 46"},{"q":"A 52-year-old patient has a burning sensation in both eyes. There has been no trauma and no contact with anyone with similar symptoms. Examination shows red, inflamed lid margins with greasy, matted lashes and dandruff-like debris at their bases. The conjunctivae are clear and there is no corneal involvement. Colonisation with which organism is found in a significant fraction of patients with this condition?","choices":["Staphylococcus aureus","Pseudomonas aeruginosa","Haemophilus influenzae","Chlamydia trachomatis"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Blepharitis is associated with S. aureus, and with rosacea and seborrhoeic dermatitis. — CMS I Common Ophthalmological Disorders, Slide 19"},{"q":"A 44-year-old fisherman has a fleshy triangular growth extending from the nasal conjunctiva ONTO the cornea of his right eye. Vision in that eye has dropped slightly. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Chemosis","Conjunctival intraepithelial neoplasia","Pinguecula","Pterygium"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The growth crosses the limbus onto the cornea, which is the whole distinction from a pinguecula. — CMS I Common Ophthalmological Disorders, Slide 28"},{"q":"A 9-year-old girl has a swollen red eyelid, proptosis, pain on eye movement and diplopia. Temperature 39.0°C. What is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Discharge on oral antibiotics with review in 48 hours","Warm compresses and observation","Admit for intravenous antibiotics and imaging","Refer to the allergy clinic"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. All post-septal disease is admitted, and imaging defines the extent. Computed tomography of the orbits and sinuses. — CMS I Common Ophthalmological Disorders, Slide 53"},{"q":"A 46-year-old man on long-term corticosteroids has glare and trouble reading, and notes his vision improves once his pupils are dilated at the clinic. Which cataract type is this?","choices":["Posterior subcapsular","Nuclear","Zonular","Cortical"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — characteristically improves with dilation, more rapid in onset, and classically under fifty. — Chronic Vision Loss & Tumors, Slide 36"},{"q":"A newborn develops a purulent conjunctivitis on day 2 of life. Which organism is most likely, given the timing?","choices":["Staphylococcus aureus","Neisseria gonorrhoeae","Chlamydia trachomatis","Herpes simplex virus"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Gonococcal disease presents in the first few days; chlamydial disease presents later, around days 5 to 14. — CMS I Common Ophthalmological Disorders, Slide 44"},{"q":"A 74-year-old woman has sudden painless loss of vision in the left eye. She has had scalp tenderness when brushing her hair and aching in the jaw when chewing for three weeks. The erythrocyte sedimentation rate is markedly raised. What is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Arrange outpatient review in two weeks","Refer for carotid imaging first","Arrange temporal artery biopsy and treat once the result is back","Start high-dose corticosteroids immediately"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Treatment precedes biopsy — the other eye is at risk within days. — 12. Acute Vision Loss, Slide 50"},{"q":"A 15-year-old girl cannot read the board at school but reads comfortably at her desk. Which refractive error is this, and how is the eye shaped?","choices":["Astigmatism, from an irregular cornea","Myopia, with the eye too long","Hyperopia, with the eye too short","Presbyopia, from a stiffening lens"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Light focuses in front of the retina, so distance is blurred and near is clear. — Chronic Vision Loss & Tumors, Slide 18"},{"q":"A 65-year-old woman is diagnosed with open-angle glaucoma. Why is she unlikely to have noticed anything?","choices":["Central vision fails first and she has adapted","The pressure is normal in all cases","The disease causes no field loss at all","Loss is gradual and the other eye compensates"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — which is why it is found on screening rather than by symptoms. — 12. Acute Vision Loss, Slide 15"},{"q":"A 54-year-old woman has painless, oval yellowish plaques on both upper lids near the inner canthus. Vision is normal. What is the most appropriate initial workup?","choices":["Computed tomography of the orbits","Fasting lipids, glucose and HbA1c","Biopsy of the lesion","Slit lamp examination alone"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The local lesion is a marker; the underlying metabolic issue is what is treated. Liver function tests are checked alongside them. — CMS I Common Ophthalmological Disorders, Slide 17"},{"q":"A 55-year-old woman with rheumatoid arthritis has severe, boring right eye pain that wakes her at night, with a violaceous hue to the sclera. Phenylephrine does not blanch the vessels. Which form carries the greatest risk of perforation?","choices":["Necrotising scleritis","Posterior scleritis","Diffuse anterior scleritis","Episcleritis"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Perforation risk is greatest in the necrotising form rather than uniformly across scleritis. — CMS I Common Ophthalmological Disorders, Slide 49"},{"q":"A 31-year-old woman has bilateral disc swelling. What distinguishes papilledema from optic neuritis on examination?","choices":["Papilledema causes a relative afferent pupillary defect","Papilledema is always unilateral","Papilledema is painful on eye movement","Acuity is preserved in papilledema and reduced in optic neuritis"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, and papilledema is bilateral while optic neuritis is usually unilateral with an afferent pupillary defect. — 12. Acute Vision Loss, Slide 45"},{"q":"A 26-year-old woman with idiopathic intracranial hypertension is started on treatment. Which measures are used?","choices":["Antibiotics","Intravitreal anti-VEGF","Weight reduction and acetazolamide","Topical antiglaucoma drops"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, with serial visual field monitoring. — Chronic Vision Loss & Tumors, Slide 5"},{"q":"Which causes of hyperopia are named besides a naturally short eye?","choices":["Chronic sun exposure","Backward lens displacement, or a mass","Diabetes alone","Prolonged near work"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — anything shortening the effective axial length. — Chronic Vision Loss & Tumors, Slide 19"},{"q":"A 29-year-old man asks for a bottle of the numbing drops that relieved his corneal abrasion pain in the emergency department. Why should he not be given them?","choices":["They cause an allergic reaction in most patients","Repeated use is toxic and delays healing","They cause permanent pupil dilation","They stain the cornea permanently"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — and it masks worsening infection. — CMS I Ocular Trauma, Slide 18"},{"q":"A 57-year-old woman has a suspected retinal detachment but the view of the fundus is poor. Which investigation is more sensitive than fundoscopy?","choices":["Fluorescein angiography","Ocular ultrasound","Gonioscopy","Tonometry"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — and it types the detachment as well. — 12. Acute Vision Loss, Slide 28"},{"q":"A 59-year-old myopic man reports a shower of new floaters and flashes of light in the right eye yesterday, followed today by a dark curtain rising across the lower half of his vision. There is no pain. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Optic neuritis","Retinal detachment","Vitreous haemorrhage","Central retinal vein occlusion"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Floaters and flashes followed by a painless curtain across the field, in a myopic patient. — 12. Acute Vision Loss, Slide 26"},{"q":"A 28-year-old woman has a sectoral area of redness in the right eye with mild discomfort but no pain and no visual change. Phenylephrine drops blanch the injected vessels. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Episcleritis","Bacterial conjunctivitis","Anterior uveitis","Scleritis"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Superficial, mildly uncomfortable, and the vessels blanch with phenylephrine. — CMS I Common Ophthalmological Disorders, Slide 47"},{"q":"A 64-year-old woman with dacryocystitis has improved on oral antibiotics. What is usually needed afterwards?","choices":["Immediate excision of the lacrimal sac","Nothing further once the infection clears","Probing and irrigation of the system","Lifelong topical antibiotics"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — the underlying nasolacrimal duct obstruction remains, and surgery may follow. To check drainage. — CMS I Common Ophthalmological Disorders, Slide 25"},{"q":"A 3-week-old infant is noted to have rhythmic to-and-fro eye movements present since birth. What is the significance of nystagmus at this age?","choices":["It resolves without evaluation by six months","It warrants evaluation for a cause of poor vision","It always indicates a brain tumour","It is a normal finding in newborns and needs no action"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Congenital nystagmus often reflects an underlying visual deficit. — 11. Neuro-Ophthalmology, Slide 4"},{"q":"A 28-year-old man has a nail protruding from his left eye after a nail gun discharged. What should be done with the nail?","choices":["Remove it under topical anaesthetic","Remove it and irrigate the tract","Remove it only once the patient is stable","Leave it in place"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. It may be tamponading the wound, and removal risks extruding intraocular contents. — CMS I Ocular Trauma, Slide 6"},{"q":"A 10-year-old girl has a white-eyed blowout fracture with entrapment. Why is urgent surgical referral required rather than routine review?","choices":["It always progresses to globe rupture","Entrapped muscle becomes ischaemic and fibroses","The eye will become infected","Vision will be lost immediately"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — delay risks permanent restriction of eye movement. — CMS I Ocular Trauma, Slide 43"},{"q":"A 19-year-old man was hit in the eye by a paintball. Examination shows blood layered in the lower part of the anterior chamber occupying about a third of it. What is the most appropriate management?","choices":["Discharge with topical antibiotics","Upright positioning, rigid shield and urgent ophthalmology review","Lie the patient flat and observe","Start aspirin for the bruising"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — keeping the head up lets the blood settle inferiorly and clear the visual axis. — CMS I Ocular Trauma, Slide 24"},{"q":"A 41-year-old woman has painful swelling of the outer upper lid and the cause is not yet clear. Why must corticosteroids not be started immediately?","choices":["Steroids are contraindicated in all lid disease","Steroids raise intraocular pressure within hours","Steroids would mask a lacrimal sac tumour","Infection must be reasonably excluded first"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — empiric oral antibiotics for 24 hours with reassessment is the alternative path. — CMS I Common Ophthalmological Disorders, Slide 23"},{"q":"Which risk factors for myopia are named?","choices":["Sun and wind exposure","Steroid use","Smoking and hypertension","Family history and near work"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, and trauma displacing the lens forward can cause it. — Chronic Vision Loss & Tumors, Slide 18"},{"q":"A 31-year-old woman has bilateral disc swelling. Which pressure is raised?","choices":["Intraocular pressure","Both equally","Neither","Intracranial pressure"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — the raised pressure is intracranial and NOT intraocular. — 12. Acute Vision Loss, Slide 42"},{"q":"A 15-year-old boy of African ancestry has a traumatic hyphema. Which screening test is particularly important in his case?","choices":["Thyroid function","Sickle cell testing","Serum calcium","Erythrocyte sedimentation rate"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Sickling in the anterior chamber obstructs outflow and raises pressure at lower blood volumes. — CMS I Ocular Trauma, Slide 25"},{"q":"Which symptoms are named in idiopathic intracranial hypertension?","choices":["Severe eye pain with nausea and halos","Jaw claudication and scalp tenderness","Headache, obscurations and intracranial noises","Itching and stringy discharge"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, with pain behind the eyes and mild pain on eye movement. — Chronic Vision Loss & Tumors, Slide 4"},{"q":"A 71-year-old man reports gradually blurring distance vision and increasing glare from oncoming headlights at night. He mentions he no longer needs his reading glasses. What explains the improvement in near vision?","choices":["A myopic shift from nuclear sclerosis","Development of hyperopia","Onset of presbyopia","Reduced corneal astigmatism"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — 'second sight'. The hardening lens increases its refractive power. — Chronic Vision Loss & Tumors, Slide 36"},{"q":"Which bones are named as sites of basilar skull fracture?","choices":["Maxilla and mandible","Parietal bones only","Cribriform plate, frontal, temporal, sphenoid","Zygoma alone"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Orbital plate of frontal, petrous or squamous temporal, and occipital. — CMS I Ocular Trauma, Slide 44"},{"q":"What holds blood in the anterior tissues in an orbital contusion?","choices":["The tarsal plate and septum act as a wall","The orbital floor","The conjunctiva","The levator muscle"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — producing preseptal ecchymosis or haematoma. — CMS I Ocular Trauma, Slide 31"},{"q":"A 26-year-old man has an orbital floor fracture with no entrapment and no ocular injury. What is the management?","choices":["Ice, analgesia and review in a few days","Immediate surgical repair","Urgent canthotomy","Admission for intravenous antibiotics"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Two to three days. — CMS I Ocular Trauma, Slide 41"},{"q":"Which local treatments for xanthelasma are named?","choices":["Lid hygiene","Topical antibiotics","Cryotherapy, laser, peel or excision","Intravitreal injection"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. — CMS I Common Ophthalmological Disorders, Slide 17"},{"q":"A 47-year-old man has proptosis, a tense orbit, severely reduced vision and a relative afferent pupillary defect one hour after facial trauma. What is the most appropriate immediate action?","choices":["Immediate enucleation","Lateral canthotomy and cantholysis","Urgent computed tomography before any intervention","Topical antiglaucoma drops alone"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — an orbital compartment syndrome needs decompression within minutes to save the nerve. — CMS I Ocular Trauma, Slide 33"},{"q":"Through which four stages does trachoma progress?","choices":["Ptosis, miosis, anhidrosis, then dilation lag","Drusen, pigment change, atrophy, then neovascularisation","Flashes, floaters, curtain, then macular involvement","Follicles, scarring, entropion, opacity"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. — CMS I Common Ophthalmological Disorders, Slide 46"},{"q":"Which tissue is inflamed in episcleritis?","choices":["The deep subconjunctival tissue","The lid margin","The iris","The sclera itself"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the episcleral layer. — CMS I Common Ophthalmological Disorders, Slide 47"},{"q":"A 7-year-old boy treated for pre-septal cellulitis returns after 48 hours with new pain on eye movement and mild proptosis. What has happened?","choices":["The infection has progressed behind the orbital septum","The swelling is simply resolving slowly","He has developed an allergic reaction to the antibiotic","The diagnosis was always a chalazion"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — painful restricted movement and proptosis mean post-septal disease. — CMS I Common Ophthalmological Disorders, Slide 53"},{"q":"A 41-year-old woman has a lid laceration 4 mm from the medial canthus after a dog bite. Why does that location matter?","choices":["It always indicates a globe rupture","The canalicular drainage may be cut","It cannot be repaired surgically","It means the levator muscle is involved"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — a missed canalicular injury leaves the patient tearing for life. — CMS I Ocular Trauma, Slide 26"},{"q":"A 64-year-old African American man presents after going blind in his right eye 'out of the blue' 20 minutes ago. There is no pain and he is not nauseated. He has had type 2 diabetes mellitus for 10 years. The left pupil reacts normally and the pressure is 17 mm Hg. The right pupil shows no reaction to light or accommodation, with a pressure of 20 mm Hg. Right ophthalmoscopy reveals arteriolar narrowing, vascular stasis and a 'boxcar' pattern. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Retinal detachment","Acute angle-closure glaucoma","Central retinal artery occlusion","Subconjunctival haemorrhage"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Sudden, painless, monocular, with segmented 'boxcar' columns in the retinal vessels. — 12. Acute Vision Loss, Slide 38"},{"q":"A 20-month-old boy has leukocoria and a convergent squint. Which two presenting features are most common in retinoblastoma?","choices":["Tearing and lid swelling","Leukocoria and strabismus","Pain and photophobia","Proptosis and fever"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — a white pupil and a squint are the two classic presentations. — Chronic Vision Loss & Tumors, Slide 41"},{"q":"Which surgical options are named for retinal detachment?","choices":["Temporal artery biopsy","Trabeculoplasty or iridotomy","Laser, cryotherapy, retinopexy or vitrectomy","Corneal transplantation"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — chosen by type. Scleral buckle as well. — 12. Acute Vision Loss, Slide 30"},{"q":"Which triad defines Horner syndrome?","choices":["Ptosis, mydriasis and diplopia","Proptosis, chemosis and diplopia","Ptosis, miosis and anhidrosis","Ptosis, exophthalmos and lid lag"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — a sympathetic pathway lesion. — 11. Neuro-Ophthalmology, Slide 27"},{"q":"A 69-year-old man with a central retinal vein occlusion is being counselled. Which complication is he monitored for?","choices":["Neovascular glaucoma","Cataract within weeks","Retinal detachment from a break","Optic neuritis"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — ischaemia drives new vessel growth at the iris and angle. — 12. Acute Vision Loss, Slide 33"},{"q":"Why is lensectomy often deferred after a full-thickness eye wall laceration?","choices":["To let the hyphema and inflammation settle","Because implants are not available acutely","Because the patient must be fasted for longer","Because the cornea must heal first for access"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. And to measure accurately for an implant. — CMS I Ocular Trauma, Slide 16"},{"q":"A 66-year-old man has amaurosis fugax from carotid emboli. Which treatments are named?","choices":["Corticosteroids alone","Intravitreal injection","Topical pressure-lowering drops","Antiplatelets, with endarterectomy"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — antiplatelet therapy for stroke risk, and surgery for the carotid source. Aspirin and clopidogrel. — 12. Acute Vision Loss, Slide 8"},{"q":"A 74-year-old man with entropion has been using preservative-free artificial tears by day and lubricating ointment at night, with the lid taped. He asks whether this will fix the problem. What should he be told?","choices":["Only surgery repositions the lid","It resolves spontaneously with age","The drops will gradually correct the lid position","Taping alone is curative if continued for six months"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The drops and taping protect the ocular surface; surgery is definitive. — CMS I Common Ophthalmological Disorders, Slide 13"},{"q":"Which form of ophthalmoscopy is described as the most commonly used?","choices":["Fluorescein angiography","Optical coherence tomography","Indirect ophthalmoscopy","Slit-lamp ophthalmoscopy"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — alongside direct and indirect methods. — CMS I Common Ophthalmological Disorders, Slide 9"},{"q":"A 59-year-old man with open-angle glaucoma is not controlled on maximal topical therapy. What is the next step?","choices":["Observation with annual review","Increase the same drops to eight times daily","Immediate enucleation","Laser trabeculoplasty"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — the named escalation when medical therapy is refractory. — 12. Acute Vision Loss, Slide 21"}]};
